#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161855 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4cac2a0-2591-548f-ba83-1c55554eb499" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "e100670a-00a3-5b89-a454-aef34af2caac" :question/math-academy-id "e-9103" :question/problem "If $X_1\\sim N(2,5)$ and $X_2\\sim N(-2, 2)$ are independent, then what is the distribution of $Y = X_1+X_2$?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2)$, then\n\n$$\nY = X_1+X_2\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma^2_1+\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nSince $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\nY = X_{1} + X_{2} \\mid ∼N(2 + (-2), 5 + 2); ∼N(0, 7)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y\\sim N(0, 7)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06dabd63-8400-57cb-8978-54f0b4640759" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "934c72e0-0fa3-55db-955a-eae8d546315e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9117" :question/problem "Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X \\sim N(2,7)$ and $Y \\sim N(5, 4)$. Find the distribution of the random variable $W = 2X-Y$." :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2)$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent, we have\n\n$$\nW = 2X - Y \\mid ∼N(2 \\cdot 2 + (-1) \\cdot 5, 2^{2} \\cdot 7 + (-1)^{2} \\cdot 4); ∼N(-1, 32)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y\\sim N(-1, 32)$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161860 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162060 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4358607d-b068-5dd9-8b35-46dc6bcecd1a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "744539e8-4bb1-57c4-acaf-ed8da68650bd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9118" :question/problem "If $X_1\\sim N(10,3^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(20,5^2)$ are independent and $Y=5X_1-4X_2$, calculate $P(3 \\lt Y \\lt 40)$. Round your final answer to two decimal places.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$$0.01$$0.02$$0.03$$\\, 1.2 \\,$ $0.8869$ $0.8888$ $0.8907$ $\\, 1.3 \\,$ $0.9049$ $0.9066$ $0.9082$ $\\, 1.4 \\,$ $0.9207$ $0.9222$ $0.9236$ |  | $z$$0.00$$0.01$$0.02$$\\, 2.7 \\,$ $0.9965$ $0.9966$ $0.9967$ $\\, 2.8 \\,$ $0.9974$ $0.9975$ $0.9976$ $\\, 2.9 \\,$ $0.9981$ $0.9982$ $0.9982$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent random variables, where $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma^2_1)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma^2_2)$, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\naX_1+bX_2\\sim N(a\\mu_1+b\\mu_2, a^2\\sigma^2_1+b^2\\sigma^2_2)\n$$\n\nSince $X_1$ and $X_2$ are independent, the distribution of $Y$ is given by\n\n$$\nY = 5X_{1} - 4X_{2} \\mid ∼N(5μ_{1} - 4μ_{2}, 5^{2} \\cdot σ_{1}^{2} + 4^{2} \\cdot σ_{2}^{2}); ∼N(5μ_{1} - 4μ_{2}, 25σ_{1}^{2} + 16σ_{2}^{2}); ∼N(5 \\cdot 10 + (-4) \\cdot 20, 25 \\cdot 3^{2} + 16 \\cdot 5^{2}); ∼N(-30, 625)\n$$\n\nTherefore, $Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = -30$ and standard deviation\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{625} = 25\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(3 \\lt Y \\lt 40)$. Thus, we convert $Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 < Y < 40) &= P((3 - (-30))/(25) < Z < (40 - (-30))/(25)) \\\\\n&= P(1.32 < Z < 2.8) \\\\\n&= P(Z < 2.8) - P(Z < 1.32) \\\\\n&= Φ(2.8) - Φ(1.32)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}Φ(1.32) & \\approx 0.9066 \\\\ Φ(2.8) & \\approx 0.9974\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(3 < Y < 40) &= Φ(2.8) - Φ(1.32) \\\\\n&\\approx 0.9974 - 0.9066 \\\\\n&\\approx 0.09\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161861 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162061 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "22f7e940-b159-5d68-8ce4-d33f4a2f6907" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Probability Involving a Sum or Difference of Normally Distributed Random Variables in Context"}] :question/id #uuid "0f84847c-0ffd-573b-ac60-c7ab7633b6ea" :question/math-academy-id "e-9119" :question/problem "The math test results of a group of students are normally distributed with a mean of $67$ and a standard deviation of $12$. The results of the same group on a history test are normally distributed with a mean of $73$ and a standard deviation of $9$. If one student is randomly selected, what is the probability their combined score from both tests is more than $150$? You may assume that the student's scores on both tests are independent.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $\\Phi(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $\\Phi(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\, 0.05 \\,$ | $\\, 0.06 \\,$ | $\\, 0.07 \\,$ | $\\, 0.08 \\,$ | $\\, 0.09 \\,$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $\\, 0.5 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7088 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7123 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7157 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7190 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7224 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.6 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7422 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7454 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7486 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7517 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7549 \\,$ |\n| $\\, 0.7 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7734 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7764 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7794 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7823 \\,$ | $\\, 0.7852 \\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ denote the student's math test result, and let $Y$ denote the student's history test result. We want to find\n\n$$\nP(X+Y > 150)\n$$\n\nSince $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables such that $X\\sim N(67,12^2)$ and $Y\\sim N(73,9^2)$, then\n\n$X + Y \\mid ∼N(67 + 73, 12^{2} + 9^{2}); ∼N(140, 225)$.\nSo, the variable $X+Y$ is normally distributed with mean $\\mu = 140$ and standard deviation\n\n$$\n\\sigma = \\sqrt{225} = 15\n$$\n\nIn order to find $P(X+Y > 150)$, we convert $X+Y$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(X + Y > 150) &= P(Z > \\frac{150 - 140}{15}) \\\\\n&\\approx P(Z > 0.67) \\\\\n&= 1 - P(Z \\le 0.67) \\\\\n&= 1 - Φ(0.67)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\Phi(0.67)\\approx 0.7486\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nP(X + Y > 150) \\mid \\approx 1 - Φ(0.67); \\approx 1 - 0.7486; = 0.2514\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that the probability that the combined score is more than $150$ is $0.2514$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162059 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "06dabd63-8400-57cb-8978-54f0b4640759" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Distribution of a Sum or Difference of Scaled Normal Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eea7485c-4e92-5771-ae00-23b15eabebbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(29, 36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158769} {:answer/id #uuid "116395f9-ad3e-56d5-8075-d97ea068aa2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(29, 38)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158770} {:answer/id #uuid "43637368-7541-5d50-b32b-52d664364b86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information to determine the answer" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158771} {:answer/id #uuid "619cf772-0ac0-57b8-960d-f6c9f6c7f047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "N(19, 36)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158772} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4da4af-97ad-56f7-951e-a378fb31fb